    formula: "Basic EPS = (net income − preferred dividends) ÷ weighted average basic shares\nDiluted EPS = adjusted net income ÷ (weighted average shares + dilutive securities)",
    example: "Net income [[$100M]], [[50M]] weighted average shares, and options adding a net [[2M]] shares under the treasury stock method. Basic EPS is $2.00; diluted EPS is $100M ÷ 52M = $1.92.",
    trap: "In a loss year, basic and diluted EPS are the same: adding shares would shrink the loss per share, so every potential share is anti-dilutive and left out."
