    topic: "Other comprehensive income",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What is other comprehensive income, and why do some gains and losses skip net income?",
    a: "Other comprehensive income collects specific unrealized gains and losses the rules keep out of net income: currency translation of foreign subsidiaries, fair-value changes on available-for-sale debt securities, effective cash-flow hedges and some pension remeasurements. They accumulate in AOCI within equity. Net income plus OCI is comprehensive income. Most items move into net income when realized.",
    why: "The rationale is volatility that says little about the period's performance. A foreign subsidiary's value in dollars swings with exchange rates, but nothing is realized unless it's sold. Running those swings through net income would bury operating results in noise, so they go straight to equity, keeping the balance sheet current and the income statement focused. When the item is realized, for example when the securities are sold, the accumulated gain or loss is reclassified into net income.",
    trap: "Equity investments are different: since 2018, US GAAP runs fair-value changes on most equity securities through net income, not OCI, so a company's earnings can swing with its stock portfolio."
