    q: "Can shareholders' equity be negative, and does that mean the company is in trouble?",
    a: "Yes, and not necessarily. Book equity turns negative when a company has lost or paid out more than shareholders put in plus what it has earned. Distress is one route, but healthy companies get there by borrowing to fund large buybacks or dividend recaps. What matters is whether cash flow comfortably covers the debt, not the sign of book equity.",
    why: "Book equity is a historical residual: assets, mostly at historical cost, minus liabilities. A strong brand or franchise generates cash but barely appears on the balance sheet, so the company can return far more cash than its book equity. Buybacks reduce equity dollar for dollar at market prices. For a highly profitable company, negative equity usually just means it has returned capital aggressively. For a loss-making one, it can mean losses have wiped out investors' capital.",
    example: "A company with [[$1.0B]] of book equity and no debt borrows [[$1.5B]] to buy back shares. Book equity falls to −$0.5B. With EBITDA of [[$600M]], leverage is 2.5x, manageable for a stable business, and the shares may still be worth several billion dollars.",
    trap: "Ratios break with negative equity: ROE and debt-to-equity become meaningless. Use debt/EBITDA, interest coverage and ROIC instead."
