    q: "What is a contra account? Give a few examples.",
    a: "A contra account offsets the account it's paired with, carrying the opposite balance. Accumulated depreciation reduces PP&E; the allowance for doubtful accounts reduces receivables; treasury stock reduces equity; sales returns and discounts reduce gross revenue. The pair is usually shown net, with the detail in the notes.",
    why: "Contra accounts keep information that netting would erase. Holding PP&E at original cost with depreciation accumulated separately shows both what the assets cost and how used up they are. Holding gross receivables with an allowance shows what customers owe and how much the company expects never to collect. The size of a contra account relative to its partner is itself a signal: a rising allowance relative to receivables can flag weakening customer credit.",
    trap: "Accumulated depreciation isn't money set aside to replace assets. It's only the cumulative expense already charged; no cash sits behind it."
